{#
    Tabs, as the element that already knows how to be a tablist.

    The core renders a `<ul>` of buttons and every panel underneath it, and
    leaves the switch to a script the renderer does not ship. So both panels
    are on the page at once and neither button does anything. This is the
    same content handed to `sds-tabs`, which draws the bar and wires the arrow
    keys.

    **The labels are a property.** A set of tabs is the one component here
    whose markup cannot tell it what it holds. The labels sit on the items
    inside it, and an element rendered before the browser has no items to
    read them off. So they stand once, on the set, the way every other option
    in this theme stands. The panels still stand between the tags, because a
    panel is a section of the document and there is no attribute that holds
    one.
#}
{%- set labels = [] -%}
{%- for tab in node.tabs -%}
    {%- set labels = labels|merge([renderNode(tab.content)|striptags|trim]) -%}
{%- endfor -%}
<sds-tabs items="{{ labels|json_encode }}">
    {%- for tab in node.tabs %}
        <sds-tab-item label="{{ labels[loop.index0] }}">
            {{- renderNode(tab.value) -}}
        </sds-tab-item>
    {%- endfor %}
</sds-tabs>
